# 2P_opto_tools Development Logic

This file records non-obvious design constraints, confirmed failure modes, and
deliberate trade-offs in this repository. Read it before changing ScanImage,
photostimulation, timing, online analysis, experiment metadata, or the update
workflow. Several implementations below are intentionally less "obvious" than
a fresh implementation might be.

This is a maintenance guide, not a protocol specification. When it conflicts
with current hardware or ScanImage source, investigate the current rig and
update this file in the same change.

## Source Of Truth

- The Python GUI/control layer is in `src/opto_schema_gui/`.
- MATLAB commands are generated in `src/opto_schema_gui/matlab_bridge.py`.
  Do not assume a Python method maps one-to-one to a MATLAB function; many are
  generated MATLAB command strings.
- ScanImage behaviour must be checked against the source tree used by the rig,
  especially `Photostim.m`, `StimulusField.m`, and `RoiGroupEditor.m`. Do not
  infer SLM behaviour only from legacy helper scripts.
- `scanimage_bridge.ini` in this repository is not the machine configuration
  source of truth. Runtime ScanImage configurations are external to this repo.

## External Machine Configuration

Machine-specific configurations intentionally live outside this Git repository.
The GUI reads their root from a per-user Windows registry setting (or a portable
setting file off Windows). There is deliberately no repository `configs/`
fallback. A missing configuration selection must prompt the user rather than
silently select an old or developer-specific configuration.

Reasons:

- MDF paths, network paths, DAQ terminals, machine launch scripts, and local
  data roots are rig-specific and must not be overwritten by application
  updates.
- Putting them in this repo previously created conflicts and made update/restart
  capable of losing or reverting machine changes.

Do not reintroduce a checked-in config fallback as a convenience feature.

Each configuration can contain multiple named ScanImage paths. The configured
`photostim_path` is authoritative for photostim actions and photostim-coordinate
conversion; it is not safe to assume `P1`, `P2`, or `PS` from a path name.
Coordinate import must load the active photostim configuration because its
objective resolution and transforms define the final target coordinates.

### Update And Restart

The update workflow treats configuration edits differently from application
code. The safe order for "Sync And Push" is:

1. Commit local configuration changes.
2. Pull/rebase remote application changes.
3. Push the rebased configuration commit.
4. Restart the application.

Pushing before pulling fails whenever the remote is ahead. Do not change this
back to push-then-pull. Untracked files are intentionally not part of this
workflow, so important configuration must be in the registered external config
repository and tracked there.

Tracked non-config application edits are discarded by Update And Restart before
pulling. This is deliberate for a rig-facing one-button updater, but it means
the button is not a development workflow. Developers must commit code changes
before using it; do not broaden the automatic config exception to arbitrary
source files.

## MATLAB Engine And ScanImage Lifecycle

Each ScanImage path is a separately launched MATLAB process. MATLAB calls
`matlab.engine.shareEngine(engine_name)` and Python attaches to that shared
engine. Python must not own MATLAB through `start_matlab()` for normal rig use.

Why:

- The MATLAB/ScanImage process must survive a GUI restart or GUI disconnect.
- Reattaching to an existing engine must not rerun the path `launch.m`, because
  that can reset ScanImage state while it is already operating.
- Stopping a path normally disconnects the GUI; it does not necessarily kill
  MATLAB.

The successful readiness condition is a usable nonempty `hSI`, not merely a
successful launcher process. Launch scripts can report an error even after
ScanImage itself starts successfully. Startup diagnostics intentionally expose
each wait stage for this reason.

There is no short fixed ScanImage-launch timeout by design. The user can cancel
the wait; automatic timeout was unreliable on slow ScanImage/MATLAB starts.

Simulation is never inferred from a failed or slow hardware launch. It must be
an explicit configured mode. Treating a failed real launch as simulation hid
hardware/configuration errors and made later state appear valid when it was not.

### Persistent GUI Operation Log

The GUI also writes a best-effort diagnostic operation log outside the repository
at the system temporary location (normally
`C:/Temp/2p_opto_tools_operations.log` on the rig, with a temp-directory fallback).
It records thread identity as well as timestamp because GUI actions, UDP handling,
and MATLAB requests can interleave. This log is intentionally independent of the
on-screen debug pane: use it after an interrupted/frozen GUI session to establish
the last completed operation.

Logging failures must never interrupt control of the microscope. Do not make
ordinary operation depend on the log file being writable, and do not move this
log into the source or external configuration repository.

## UDP Contracts And Experiment Metadata

There are two inbound control forms:

- Legacy MATLAB-serialized `COM/GOGO/STOP` messages, replied to with `READY`.
- JSON commands, including `update_experiment_params`, `prep_patterns`,
  `trigger_photo_stim`, and `check_idle`.

UDP parsing, state tracking, and replies stay in Python. MATLAB should receive
only the required actions/context. Moving protocol parsing into MATLAB would
make ordering, error reporting, and controller replies harder to make safe.

### Large Experiment Descriptions

Full experiment/trial descriptions can exceed a UDP datagram. The supported
workflow is for the controller to write the JSON to the NAS experiment folder
and send a small JSON notification containing `params_path`. The GUI resolves
the Windows UNC path to the Linux NAS mount when required, reads the file, and
checks that its `expID` agrees with the UDP notification.

Do not put the complete trial list back into a UDP payload. The nominal maximum
UDP datagram size is not a safe application payload limit, and truncation or
network fragmentation produces opaque failures.

### Schema Name And Trial List

`schema_name` is carried in the `opto_2p` feature parameters of stimulus
conditions. There is intentionally no top-level schema definition to fall back
to. On experiment update the code requires every opto condition to use the same
schema name. The first opto condition provides the selected name; disagreement
is an error.

This is necessary because the full trial order is used for phase-mask batching
and the active ScanImage sequence. A mixed-schema trial list cannot safely be
prepared as one continuous photostim run.

### Experiment Schema Snapshot

When a validated opto experiment is received, the resolved shared schema is
copied atomically to `<NAS experiment root>/schema.yaml`. The experiment root
is created if it does not yet exist.

- An identical existing snapshot is accepted.
- A different existing snapshot is an error, not an overwrite.

This is intentionally strict: the shared schema library can later change, but
the experiment must retain an immutable provenance record. Do not replace this
with a symbolic reference to the shared schema path.

## New Experiment State Reset

Starting a new `expID` clears existing ScanImage photostim groups and online
analysis/integration ROIs. This avoids accidental reuse of patterns, phase
masks, or ROI traces from a prior experiment.

This reset happens only when the experiment ID changes. Do not clear state on
ordinary trial updates within the same experiment: that would destroy the
prebuilt active photostim sequence and its phase-mask state.

## Schema Validation Before ScanImage Mutation

Schema preflight validation intentionally happens in Python before clearing,
creating, or starting anything in ScanImage. Empty sequences, patterns without
cells, missing pattern references, bad sequence indices, and invalid timing
must fail before ScanImage is mutated.

The GUI also prevents saving schemas with empty patterns or sequences. Do not
relax this merely to allow draft schema files: an invalid schema after existing
groups have been cleared leaves a live rig in an ambiguous state.

### Normalize Controller Data At The Boundary

The upstream controller may serialise a single stimulus condition differently
from a list of conditions. Python normalises these JSON shape variations when
the experiment parameters are received, before schema validation, online-analysis
configuration, or photostim preparation. Downstream code must operate on the
normalised trial-condition list rather than assuming a field is always a list.

This is particularly important for one-condition experiments: treating a scalar
condition object as an iterable has previously produced a misleading preparation
or online-analysis failure only in that otherwise valid case.

## Coordinate And Depth Conventions

### SLM XY Coordinates

Schema/manual target coordinates are in microns. ScanImage `StimulusField`
stores `centerXY` in reference coordinates, so XY microns are divided by
`hSI.objectiveResolution` before assignment.

This repository targets the SI_2026 ScanImage coordinate model. In this version
`slmPattern` XY is in **absolute reference coordinates**; `centerXY` controls
the galvo/log-spiral position independently. The stored row is
`[x_ref, y_ref, z, weight]`, and point weights must be in `[0, 1]`.

Do not apply the older ScanImage rule that subtracts `centerXY` from SLM XY.
That rule existed in older RoiGroupEditor implementations but would double-shift
points on SI_2026. The MATLAB builder has an explicit comment beside the
absolute assignment in `prepareSchemaPhotostim.m`; preserve that convention
unless the installed ScanImage source proves it has changed again.

The authoritative implementation is the actual ScanImage version installed on
the rig, not an old test script or another SI checkout. Inspect its
RoiGroupEditor/StimulusField implementation before changing this logic.

### Pattern Power

The schema's pattern power is **power per cell**, not total group power. Before
creating a ScanImage stimulus field, the code scales it by the number of cells
in that pattern. Import uses the same meaning. Do not rename/reinterpret this
as a global group power without changing both schema import and MATLAB pattern
generation.

`power_scale` remains per cell. It becomes the SLM point weight; if all supplied
weights are zero, the MATLAB builder deliberately replaces them with one so an
otherwise valid target pattern is not converted into a pathological zero-weight
SLM mask. Laser power is separately forced to zero for intentional off/gap
epochs.

### Two Distinct Z Values

For imaging-derived cells:

- `cell.z` is the true photostimulation target Z.
- `cell.origin_z_um` is the nominal ScanImage imaging-plane Z.

Integration ROI placement must use `origin_z_um`; photostimulation targets use
`cell.z`. They are equal for a stepped slow stack but can differ for fast-Z
continuous acquisition. Collapsing them breaks either stimulation targeting or
imaging-plane ROI ownership.

For fast-Z, true Z is inferred from acquisition order and the pixel Y position
within the continuous volume sweep. It is deliberately not treated as a flat
per-plane Z value.

### Processed-Cell Import Metadata Is Deliberately Strict

Importing cells from processed Suite2p data requires a
`*_selectedScanfield.roi` file. TIFF-header metadata is intentionally not used
as a fallback: it was too ambiguous for reliable mapping from processed cells
to the selected ScanImage scanfield. If that ROI file is missing, import must
fail visibly rather than make a plausible-looking but potentially wrong target.

On Windows the processed-data root is
`F:/Local_Repository_Processed/<animalID>/<expID>`. On Linux, an explicit
source user resolves through `/home/<user>/data/Repository/...`. Do not add
`tif_meso`, arbitrary local repositories, or a broad TIFF-header fallback to
"make imports work"; selecting the wrong experiment's geometry is worse than a
clear import error.

### MATLAB YAML Field Names

YAML pattern and sequence names are user-facing names and may not be valid
MATLAB struct field names. MATLAB loading applies `matlab.lang.makeValidName`,
but the schema loaders retain/use the original `name` field when enumerating or
resolving items. Do not access YAML-derived MATLAB structs solely by a raw
human-readable name; use the supplied original-name lookup helpers.

## ScanImage Photostim Groups, SLM Masks, And Sequences

### Do Not Mutate A Live Active Sequence

Earlier implementations appended or edited `hPs.sequenceSelectedStimuli` while
ScanImage was active. That eventually produced a failure where the sequence
advanced but later groups lost their SLM phase-mask behaviour and appeared as a
central spiral/point. The exact ScanImage internal failure is not fully
observable, but it is repeatable enough to be a hard constraint.

The current design precomposes the needed sequence before starting photostim.
At trial time, `build_trigger_photostim_command()` only verifies that the next
tail is already present at the current sequence position (or one position
ahead). It does not append or replace a live tail.

Do not "simplify" this by appending the next trial on demand, even if it looks
more memory efficient.

### Full Trial Order Is Required

The controller must provide the complete `trial_condition_indices` list before
photostim preparation. This permits all required sequence groups to be
precomposed and avoids editing the active ScanImage sequence later.

Non-opto trials remain in the planned list as `None` for the photostim sequence
mapping. They are not errors and must not cause mask generation or online ROI
trial plotting.

### Reserved Groups

Groups 1 and 2 are reserved as `BLANK` and `PARK`, respectively. Prepared
schema sequence groups start at 3. Do not renumber these casually: Python's
sequence/group mapping and generated MATLAB commands rely on this convention.

The standard prepared trial tail ends with `PARK` (group 2) so ScanImage reaches
a known terminal idle state. It should not have redundant leading/trailing
parks. The waveform pulse count must exactly match the group sequence it is
advancing; changing one without the other causes timing drift or a stuck
sequence.

### Completion Means Reaching The Tracked Terminal PARK

`check_idle` does not treat a generic ScanImage `active`/`running` flag as proof
that a trial has completed. When a trial sequence is inserted, Python records
both its insert position and its final idle-PARK position. A trial is complete
only when the sequence cursor has reached that recorded terminal PARK (or a
batch regeneration state explicitly reports that it is still busy).

This distinction prevents the upstream controller from starting the next trial
while ScanImage has merely changed state between groups. Preserve the marker
output and position bookkeeping if sequence construction changes; replacing it
with a fixed sleep or a bare `hPs.active` test reintroduces the intermittent
retrigger and mask-loading races that led to this design.

### Explicit Off Epochs Must Keep A Real Pattern Shape

Gaps are not represented by an empty or NaN-only ScanImage stimulus ROI. Those
forms have produced invalid AO/SLM state in ScanImage. Instead, a gap uses the
next applicable schema pattern's geometry with all beam powers forced to zero.

This can look wasteful because the SLM/galvo pattern still exists during a gap,
but it ensures ScanImage receives a valid stimulus field and only the beam is
off. Do not replace it with an empty group, NaN coordinates, or a different
unrelated point pattern without retesting `hPs.start()`.

### SLM Epoch Limitation In This ScanImage Build

On the rig's customised ScanImage build, a single stimulus group containing
multiple distinct SLM epochs can fail inside ScanImage AO generation when
`hPs.start()` is called. The core code assumes a scalar SLM mask efficiency,
but multiple epochs yield a struct array.

Therefore sequences are split into fixed-duration stimulus groups rather than
one giant "whole sequence" group. Do not merge all sequence steps into a single
group merely to reduce trigger count.

### Block Duration

`sequence_block_duration_s` defines how schema playback is partitioned into
ScanImage groups. Timing and waveform trigger positions are derived from the
same partition. It is a rig/config parameter, not a cosmetic GUI setting.

Any alteration must update all of these consistently:

- MATLAB sequence-window construction.
- Python mapping from schema sequence to stimulus groups.
- Generated waveform pulse times.
- Controller expectations for an idle terminal park.

### Spiral Centre Is Intentionally Offset

Normal schema stimulation uses `logspiral`, not a static point function. The
spiral centre is chosen by `chooseSpiralCenter()` with the configured
`min_center_distance_um` constraint, rather than always being `[0, 0]` or the
mean of target points. This protects targets from the central/zero-order region
while the SLM points remain at their requested locations.

Therefore a nonzero `StimulusField.centerXY` is not evidence that target
coordinates were transformed incorrectly. `point` is reserved for zero-power
park/test cases; changing normal schema patterns to `point` removes intended
spiral stimulation.

### Photostim Monitoring Is Optional

`hSI.hPhotostim.monitoring` (ScanImage "Show") and `.logging` ("Log") are
specific to photostim galvo/beam monitoring, not TIFF logging or integration
ROI analysis. They start extra analog acquisition, logging, and display work.

The application deliberately does **not** force them on during normal schema
preparation. Users may enable them in ScanImage when needed for diagnosis.
Do not restore unconditional enabling: it added acquisition load and is not
needed for stimulation, phase masks, or online plotting.

## Phase-Mask Batch Limit

The SLM cannot safely precompute unlimited unique phase masks. The "Phase mask
batch size" control is a maximum number of unique **schema pattern names**, not
a number of stimulus groups or trials. A repeated pattern used by multiple
groups counts once.

The batch planner walks the complete planned trial order and includes as many
consecutive trials as fit under that unique-pattern limit. It deliberately
prepares batches by pattern identity, not by group count.

The full order must therefore arrive before preparation begins. A request to
prepare an individual sequence without the experiment parameters is rejected:
guessing a batch from the currently selected trial makes phase-mask reuse and
the later batch boundary unknowable.

At the boundary the controller must continue to see the system as non-idle:
`check_idle` starts preparation of the next batch and reports an appropriate
non-idle/preparing state. This is how the upstream controller waits without
starting a next trial while phase masks are regenerated.

Do not acknowledge the end of the last prepared trial as idle before the next
batch is ready. Do not restart the raw trial waveform for a mask batch; it is a
per-trial trigger task and must remain tied to trial execution, not mask setup.

## Trial Timing And Digital Waveform

Time zero for online analysis is the external input that starts the digital
waveform task, not controller arm time and not the first later photostim pulse.
The waveform task drives the configured output line, which advances ScanImage's
photostim sequence through the prepared groups.

The external trial edge is detected by a callback on that vDAQ task. On that
callback, the code captures the current integration ROI cursors, frame numbers,
and timestamps so trial alignment is based on ScanImage's own data clock.

### Callback Rate Is Deliberately Bounded

The waveform sample rate may be MHz. `sampleCallbackN` must never be 1: that
creates a MATLAB callback for every digital sample (up to millions per second),
starves ScanImage acquisition processing, and can freeze/stutter imaging at
trial onset.

The current callback interval is approximately 5 ms (200 Hz), independent of
the raw waveform sample rate. It is sufficient for onset detection and avoids
the runaway MATLAB event load. Do not change this to one callback per sample.

### vDAQ Terminal Direction Matters

On this rig, `D3.x` is output-only, `D2.x` input-only, and `D0.x`/`D1.x` can
be configured either way but not simultaneously. A digital output does not
magically become a valid ScanImage trigger input; physical/internal routing and
terminal direction must match the configured waveform output, external-start
input, and `hPs.stimTriggerTerm`.

If photostim does not advance, verify wiring/routing and terminal direction
before changing sequence code.

## Online Analysis And Integration ROIs

### ScanImage Produces The Values

The GUI does not calculate fluorescence from TIFF frames. ScanImage's
`hIntegrationRoiManager` calculates values on its MATLAB acquisition thread and
stores values, timestamps, frame numbers, and per-ROI circular cursors.

The generated online-analysis ROIs are single-plane `IntegrationField`s,
therefore normally yield one value per acquired imaging frame for their owning
imaging plane. A multi-plane integration ROI would intentionally yield one
value only when its final slice/volume is complete.

### Missing Points Are Not Necessarily A GUI Bug

ScanImage performs integration, motion estimation, and related processing in
the MATLAB acquisition path. If this work does not complete before a subsequent
frame arrives, ScanImage can drop integration processing for a frame while
still writing imaging data to disk. A jump in integration frame numbers is
therefore real upstream information, not something the GUI can interpolate
truthfully.

The GUI polls the circular history using cursors. It receives every new value
created since the previous poll; it does not only read the latest value. The
10 Hz poll rate is intentional: it lowers MATLAB Engine contention while cursor
deltas preserve all generated samples. Do not increase polling frequency to
"fix" gaps without proving the acquisition thread can tolerate the extra
MATLAB work.

The MATLAB delta command snapshots each history matrix once per poll before
extracting every ROI's cursor range. Do not revert to repeated property reads
inside the per-ROI loop; that scales badly with ROI count.

### Online ROI Scope

Integration ROIs are built for the union of unique cells across all opto
conditions. ScanImage collects those ROI values continuously while imaging.
For a non-opto trial, no photostim mask is generated and no online-analysis
trial response is opened/plotted, even though the underlying integration stream
may still exist.

### Trial Alignment

The online plot uses ScanImage timestamps and frame numbers plus the snapshot
taken when the external waveform starts. It must not use controller receive
time, photostim arm time, or an inferred first artifact as time zero.

Each ROI may have a slightly different latest cursor/timestamp at time zero.
That is expected; the code records them per ROI. Do not replace this with one
global cursor unless ScanImage changes its integration manager semantics.

### Exported Raw Responses

The live plotting cache intentionally retains only a bounded number of trials,
but response export must retain the entire experiment. Each completed trial is
therefore appended immediately to a JSONL staging file in the experiment's
`online_analysis` directory. On experiment stop, path shutdown, or replacement
by a new experiment, that staging file is atomically assembled into
`online_analysis_responses.json`. Do not use the display cache as the export
source.

The exported samples retain their original ScanImage timestamps, frame numbers,
and values. Trial lengths may differ, so analysis code must use the supplied
time base and leave bins outside a trial's observed range as NaN rather than
padding or extrapolating values.

`CellSpec.z` is the SLM stimulation Z. `origin_z_um` is the source imaging
plane Z, which can differ. Export metadata deliberately labels these as
`stimulation_coordinates_um` and `integration_coordinates_um`; do not merge
them into a single ambiguous coordinate field.

### dF/F And Plot Gaps

For dF/F, the GUI smooths the available trace and uses its 10th percentile over
the pre-stimulus interval as F0 (falling back to all available data if there is
no pre-stimulus section). This is intentionally robust against elevated
pre-stim activity.

Non-finite samples are removed before raw plotting. This means a NaN inside a
trace is visually connected across by the current polyline. Averaging first
interpolates each available series onto a common grid; regions outside a
series' observed time span remain NaN and are excluded by `nanmean`.

This behaviour is a display decision, not data repair. Do not infer that a
visually continuous line proves every frame was integrated.

### All-Conditions View

The Online Activity selector defaults to "All conditions." That view tiles
supported condition panels into a near-square grid, shows all tracked cells in
each panel, and uses dF/F regardless of the single-view normalization selector.

- Green cells are associated with that condition; blue cells are other tracked
  cells.
- The active condition has a green border and includes the live trace.
- All panels share one scale, rounded outward to 0.5 increments.
- Each cell row marks and labels zero and the shared upper scale.
- Mean traces have shaded SEM bands.

The single-condition view is intentionally different: it includes only cells
associated with that condition and only trials of that condition. "All traces"
means all repeats of the selected condition, not all conditions.

## ScanImage Integration Performance

The scanner hardware remains timed even when the display appears to stutter.
ScanImage's MATLAB frame path performs display/data-manager work, motion
estimation, photostim motion compensation, integration ROI calculation, and
user-function notification. Load in any of these can make display or
integration updates irregular.

Before changing integration logic to chase missing points, distinguish:

- continuous TIFF frame sequence with jumps in integration frame numbers:
  MATLAB integration processing is behind;
- gaps in TIFFs too: investigate hardware, vDAQ/PCIe, logging, acquisition
  settings, or disk throughput.

The prior trial-waveform callback bug was one cause of severe load. Photostim
monitoring/logging and overly frequent Engine requests are other avoidable
loads. Keep this pipeline lightweight during high-rate imaging.

## Pattern Test And Diagnostics Operations

"Send pattern to ScanImage" is a destructive testing operation: it clears
existing photostim groups **and** online-analysis/integration ROIs before
constructing the test pattern. This prevents stale state from appearing to be
part of the test. It is not an experiment-start action and should not be used
while relying on existing online analysis.

The manual test pattern differs from normal experiment playback: it is a
single group with the test pattern rather than normal fixed block splitting.
Its blank/pause and park semantics are deliberately constructed for manual
inspection. Do not use it as a reference for runtime experiment sequence
construction.

SLM PSF diagnostics save and restore a broad set of ScanImage state (motor,
stack, imaging/logging, motion, and photostim settings). Diagnostic stack Z
must be configured by first moving the stage motor to the desired centre, then
using a centered uniform stack. Do not calculate arbitrary stage positions from
an assumed global Z origin.

## Repository Hygiene

- Do not commit `__pycache__` or `.pyc` files.
- Keep application code ASCII unless the relevant existing file requires
  otherwise.
- Do not change or discard unrecognized dirty files while working; they may be
  user changes or generated rig state.
- Commit and push completed changes so remote testing runs the tested code.

## Suggested Investigation Order

For a photostim or SLM regression:

1. Confirm the active external machine configuration and hardware terminal
   routing.
2. Inspect current `hSI.hPhotostim` state and the prepared
   `sequenceSelectedStimuli` without mutating it.
3. Verify the complete trial order, schema name consistency, and phase-mask
   batch plan.
4. Check the actual ScanImage source version before changing group/SLM logic.
5. Use generated debug markers and the persistent operation log rather than
   adding broad unconditional `disp` output.
6. Preserve the existing sequence/mask lifecycle unless a controlled test
   proves a different ScanImage version has changed the constraint.
